      <div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:01</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay. My name is Carrie Tarasuk. I&#39;m the Assistant Curator of Collections at the First Division Museum at Cantigny. We&#39;re here at the First Division Reunion in St. Louis, Missouri. on Friday, August 10th, 2007.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:14</div><div class="transcript-line">And we&#39;re doing an oral history with I&#39;m Clark Welch. Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">00:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And I&#39;m Ray Albin. Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:23</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay, um my first question for both of you is the Battle of Ang Tan was part of Operation Shenandoah. And I know that there was Shenandoah 1 and 2. Can you explain that a little better, what the purpose of Operation Shenandoah was and just give me a better understanding of that?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:43</div><div class="transcript-line">My my answer is not gonna be very good. I didn&#39;t have any idea what operation we were on or what it was called.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:50</div><div class="transcript-line">I didn&#39;t even know it was the Battle of Ong Tom until after it was over. And I really didn&#39;t know until I read in newspapers that it was part of Shenandoah. I&#39;m sure that there was a good purpose for it, but I had no idea what operation we were on.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:04</div><div class="transcript-line">And conversely, usually the information got down through command level to the enlisted men. And somehow, because I recall a letter I wrote, to a girl and uh it indicated that we were on operation Shenandoah II. I actually gave her the grid coordinates of where we were.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23</div><div class="transcript-line">at On Tan and said that if she had a map she could find it. It&#39;s in the book by David. And uh I knew it was Shenandoah too, but even after the battle I remember uh a chaplain referring to it in the service back at Lycay as the Battle of the 17th.</div><div class="timestamp">01:40</div><div class="transcript-line">I think I indicated that to you yesterday. And only with David Marinus&#39;s or Moranus&#39;s book Did I learn that it was called the Battle of Long Tan? When that name came on board, I I don&#39;t know.</div><div class="timestamp">01:53</div><div class="transcript-line">But I did know we were on Operation Shenandoah too, but I didn&#39;t have any other particulars as to who the enemy was and</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:02</div><div class="transcript-line">So you just knew it was referred to as Apparation Shenandoah and didn&#39;t remember anything else about it.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:13</div><div class="transcript-line">you know as difficult as we assumed probably and more so. Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:18</div><div class="transcript-line">Um I wanted to ask what do you think of um Terry Allen Jr. and How do you how do you think how how do you think he felt about the plan for that day? From the reading that I&#39;ve done, you know, it sounded like he was following orders and maybe not saying much about it, but how do you think he personally felt</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Again, my answer is uh he was my battalion commander and uh a good army guy, no matter what level you do what your boss says So my uh every thought about Terry Allen, I didn&#39;t know his first name. I knew he was Colonel Allen. I did not know he was famous.</div><div class="timestamp">03:01</div><div class="transcript-line">I didn&#39;t know his father was famous. I didn&#39;t, you know, y you guys weren&#39;t there with your books I didn&#39;t know that he had three daughters. I knew that he had some kind of problem, but hell everybody had problems.</div><div class="timestamp">03:14</div><div class="transcript-line">What I knew of him was that he was a a good, fine gentleman. Uh had no business being out there in the middle of a war in the middle of a jungle, but a good, honest, fine gentleman. And it was uh very confusing for me when I would hear him say things that didn&#39;t make sense, because I you expect a good fine man, but I didn&#39;t know anything about him being uh important being famous.</div><div class="timestamp">03:45</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh and I really didn&#39;t have a very good feeling for any pressure he was under. He didn&#39;t, no I I say he didn&#39;t. I was present when he briefed the generals, although I think just one general was there and some colonels to include Major Hollander and some colonels on the evening of the 16th. And I I didn&#39;t see any, when I was present, I didn&#39;t see any pressure.</div><div class="timestamp">04:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I saw the colonel, my boss, talking about what a wonderful job we&#39;d done the 16th, and we had done good the 16th. And uh the colonel saying, yep, we&#39;ll go back in and get them tomorrow. A good idea.</div><div class="timestamp">04:26</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh I didn&#39;t, but I I wasn&#39;t privy to what the gentleman, the colonel, had to say. I was not aware of any pressure on him until while the battle was going on. It was obvious to me that He was on the phone, but the person on the other end of the phone wasn&#39;t listening.</div><div class="timestamp">04:45</div><div class="transcript-line">Not a very good answer yet.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">04:48</div><div class="transcript-line">Honest one.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">04:51</div><div class="transcript-line">And as a sergeant in the border platoon at that time, I had absolutely no contact with Colonel Allen. I knew just through my chain of command and the fact that I had to know that Colonel Allen was the battalion commander. I didn&#39;t have any inkling of you know, his being related to Terry Allen Senior or that he had any kind of problems.</div><div class="timestamp">05:11</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh he was my battalion commander and uh I knew less about him than Colonel Welch did. Okay. And that&#39;s just the way you know it was.</div><div class="timestamp">05:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean, enlisted men did not know that much about that.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">05:26</div><div class="transcript-line">Right.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">05:28</div><div class="transcript-line">Jim Shelton would be the only man that I know of that would know what Colonel Allen was like. Jim Shelton was Colonel Allen&#39;s friend, only friend. No, I don&#39;t want to say only friend.</div><div class="timestamp">05:42</div><div class="transcript-line">No, no, but he was a good friend. Intimate that yeah. And Jim Shelton knew exactly what he&#39;s doing, what he&#39;s thinking.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">05:53</div><div class="transcript-line">I was I was hoping we have um we already have an oral history with you um recounting some of the you know the events of the battle but I thought it would be very interesting and very helpful if both of you could could recount kind of what happened on the 17th together</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">06:16</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;ll be from different perspectives because absolutely, but it should be good. It should it should match.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">06:23</div><div class="transcript-line">It should different perspectives, however, because I was not at the ambush site, I was in the weapons or mortar platoon and then made it to the I&#39;m gonna call it an ambush. I don&#39;t care. Ambush site. Good words.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">06:35</div><div class="transcript-line">It was an attack on a fortified position. Yes. We just didn&#39;t know that.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">06:38</div><div class="transcript-line">So my mine would be a completely different perspective up until the point where I made it with I don&#39;t know what you want to call it rescue people or whatever. I don&#39;t know what we were rescuing, but rescue people there. And so You know, Colonel Welch obviously has more the preponderance of the information.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">06:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Everybody that was in my uh morta plutone. were smart people. We were all infantrymen, so we&#39;re whatever we were.</div><div class="timestamp">07:04</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean, we&#39;re just guys out there in the middle of the woods. But from the platoon leader, Fred Mager, who died just recently. On down to the lowest uh uh private uh I don&#39;t I don&#39;t uh they were smart guys that was selected for that, that they had to do math, they had to do figuring.</div><div class="timestamp">07:21</div><div class="transcript-line">We didn&#39;t have to do figuring. And uh Long story, of course, but to make it short, on the 16th, my mortar, my, I&#39;m not bragging, I&#39;m saying my I love my mortar platoon did Almost textbook perfect. All the way out on the 16th, they fired to the left, they fired to the right because mortars Are good mortars sometimes fire too short or too long, but mortars very seldom fire wrong to the left or far wrong to the right.</div><div class="timestamp">07:50</div><div class="transcript-line">So while we walked out The mortars fired to our left and right, the artillery fired to our front. It was almost like an exercise. And anything I&#39;m not sure who I talked to when I called back.</div><div class="timestamp">08:01</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh sometimes I called back, sometimes my sergeants would call back, but the uh Everything uh on the 16th, nothing&#39;s perfect, but it was perfect. Uh no screaming voices when when you talk back to uh you know fire alpha three, fire alpha seven, uh you just get Roger on the way. On the 16th, it was almost perfect.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">08:22</div><div class="transcript-line">I wonder if I could just interrupt for a second. Sorry. But we were if we were almost perfect, it was because we had trained that way.</div><div class="timestamp">08:30</div><div class="transcript-line">prior to going into showing. trust their mortars. Okay.</div><div class="timestamp">08:44</div><div class="transcript-line">But that was no accident that we were prepared on the 16th because we had prepared ourselves or had a we Clark or Lieutenant Welch or Colonel Welch had prepared us.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">08:52</div><div class="transcript-line">We worked hard on uh I was very fortunate. I was given a couple weeks to train together before we had to go out and fight. The division did have a lot of rules because mortars are Mortars are dangerous.</div><div class="timestamp">09:05</div><div class="transcript-line">If a mortar went off in this room, it&#39;d kill all of us. Mortars are dangerous. So the division did have a lot of rules.</div><div class="timestamp">09:12</div><div class="transcript-line">Have to be this close, have to be this far away, have to do this. And we worked hard at uh making sure you obey the r you can&#39;t disobey the rules, but as an example, you can&#39;t shoot until admission is approved. So when I woke up in the morning, 4 o&#39;clock, 5 o&#39;clock, whatever it was, I was call the mortars and I would say, I got a mission for you.</div><div class="timestamp">09:35</div><div class="transcript-line">Fire one round right out there. And boom, that&#39;s that&#39;s the mission. Now the mission is approved.</div><div class="timestamp">09:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Sometimes it would take, I&#39;d I&#39;d wake up at four, uh five, wake up at five, I would call the artillery and I&#39;d call it easy to call the motors, they&#39;re just laying right over there. And I would say, I&#39;ve got a new mission for you, fire. And we get, then they have to get all these approvals.</div><div class="timestamp">09:56</div><div class="transcript-line">You have to get approvals to mortar shoots high, you have to make sure no airplanes are around. Just bullshit. So sometimes it take two or three minutes, sometimes it take two hours to get approval.</div><div class="timestamp">10:07</div><div class="transcript-line">But the point was, by the time it came time to do any fighting, We already had approval. The mortars had started that mission. They&#39;d f fired one round and then maybe go the whole day without firing again.</div><div class="timestamp">10:19</div><div class="transcript-line">And and the the distances, you know, you you have to know where everybody is. I had designated smoke because the people up in the helicopter I knew where everybody was. They knew where everybody was.</div><div class="timestamp">10:30</div><div class="transcript-line">They wouldn&#39;t have shot on me. But the people who&#39;d fly over in the helicopters would say, Where are you? So I had des one man on each platoon that was called a smoke man.</div><div class="timestamp">10:39</div><div class="transcript-line">And if the general expected me to be over by that road junction, but I wasn&#39;t over there because you go to road junk and you get killed, and the general would fly over said and said, How come you&#39;re not at the road junction? Oh sir, I am. And then he will say, well smoke, throw smoke.</div><div class="timestamp">10:56</div><div class="transcript-line">I want to know where you are. So I would designate First Platoon. Run out to that road junction and throw smoke because some fruitcake up there wants us to be at the road junction.</div><div class="timestamp">11:06</div><div class="transcript-line">So the the guy would leave all his equipment behind, pick up a can of smoke and run out there and throw the thing and come running back in and hope nobody shot him. So we we you follow the rules, but the uh these guys, they were good. We&#39;d had a couple of accidents back in uh their first platoon sergeant W some of the first rounds we ever fired back at our base camp when we were shooting, we were living, it&#39;s in a tent with a dirt floor, but it&#39;s a place to live.</div><div class="timestamp">11:34</div><div class="transcript-line">In the mortars were out there we cut down thousands of dollars worth of rubber trees to give these guys a place to fire. It paid back the vehicle. Somebody had to pay.</div><div class="timestamp">11:42</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t I don&#39;t know. But so man, they raised hell when they came in there. We we&#39;d cut down all these rubber trees.</div><div class="timestamp">11:49</div><div class="transcript-line">So these are guys have a place to shoot, and then we use the rubber trees and cut them into ten foot-long places and they were the overhead cover for the bunkers. I thought it was really good Some guy came down there store a check and I think we owed him $400,000 or something.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">12:01</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t care. This was back in Lycate.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">12:04</div><div class="transcript-line">But we would shoot at night. We&#39;d shoot. We&#39;re we&#39;re defending this position, not defending, because the enemy&#39;s out there. We shoot over their heads. But one night it w a round and I heard when it fired, it w you know mortar rounds are supposed to go whoop and this one went boop.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">12:19</div><div class="transcript-line">Yep</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">12:20</div><div class="transcript-line">And I came running down there and what had happened was the uh because of a defect in the factory, the tail had come off the mortar round. So when the mortar round came out, it didn&#39;t have a tail on it that stayed a tube or something. Right or or whatever it did.</div><div class="timestamp">12:34</div><div class="transcript-line">So the mortar round came out and tumbled, so it didn&#39;t go way out there. It went back here and it landed close to one of our own guys and wounded somebody, I think. So as I came running out to the p uh mortars to see what had happened, the first sergeant went out to the man that had been hurt, and literally my first sergeant, who uh my beloved first sergeant, kept the man alive.</div><div class="timestamp">12:56</div><div class="transcript-line">So he didn&#39;t die, but he was hurt. And when I came back to the mortar platoon, the platoon sergeant back there had a compass because he knew that he&#39;d done everything right and he&#39;s going to walk out there into enemy territory. to find out what went wrong with it.</div><div class="timestamp">13:10</div><div class="transcript-line">He guessed that the tail came off. But if the tail came off, he said, sir, I&#39;ll find it. I&#39;ll find the goddamn thing.</div><div class="timestamp">13:15</div><div class="transcript-line">It wasn&#39;t our fault. It wasn&#39;t our fault. And it wasn&#39;t him trying to get out of Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">13:20</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, not what it was him just they were awfully good. They were awfully good. Now When I came back in on the 16th, I went to the mortar platoon first to talk to them.</div><div class="timestamp">13:34</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t know if I shook their hand, but we probably drank a cup of coffee. The mortars were Wonderful, wonderful, wonderful. And the mortars would often come out with us.</div><div class="timestamp">13:44</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh I would usually take one or two sergeants from the mortar platoon who would walk with us, because that way they could talk back and the guys Like to get a chance to come out with us is kind of exciting out there. And the most of the guys who were mortarmen had walked with us. Uh it wasn&#39;t that you were in the mortars, so therefore you stayed back in the Most of them, so I knew they were very, very good now in the 17th.</div><div class="timestamp">14:12</div><div class="transcript-line">When I was told that I would not be the lead company And the lead company, it&#39;s always good to be up front. You know, you don&#39;t want to be picking up somebody else&#39;s crap. When I was told I wouldn&#39;t be the lead company, the most important thing was that I wouldn&#39;t get to fire the artillery.</div><div class="timestamp">14:29</div><div class="transcript-line">I wouldn&#39;t get to fire the mortars. That&#39;s a good rule. You get two people moving through the woods.</div><div class="timestamp">14:34</div><div class="transcript-line">If this guy starts firing, he&#39;s liable to shoot up the guy in front. So the rule is only the guy in front can fire. Fine.</div><div class="timestamp">14:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Now the same way that this other stuff, I went ahead and I sat down with the mortars and I sat, well you call artillery on the telephone. With my forward observer. And we made plans for the 17th, just like the 16th.</div><div class="timestamp">15:00</div><div class="transcript-line">On the way out, Alpha 1, Alpha 2, Alpha 3, Alpha 4, Alpha 5, those are the targets. Bravo 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. What if I get hit here?</div><div class="timestamp">15:09</div><div class="transcript-line">So we&#39;ll set up a Delta 127. What if I get hit here? What if we want the enemy to think we&#39;re going that direction, so we&#39;ll fire some mortars off that way So the enemy will think we&#39;re going in that direction.</div><div class="timestamp">15:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I made all of those plans, knowing that we weren&#39;t going to be allowed to fire. But we made all the plans. And when we walked out, and we started to walk at 8 o&#39;clock in the morning and from 8 till 10 o&#39;clock, when we walked, I was constantly talking to these guys, saying, I know we can&#39;t fire because we&#39;re not the guys up front.</div><div class="timestamp">15:40</div><div class="transcript-line">However If we could, you&#39;d now fire Alpha 1. And no one fired between 8 and 10. Nobody because the lead company, because we were sneaking up on them, no one fired.</div><div class="timestamp">15:54</div><div class="transcript-line">The artillery didn&#39;t fire. The Air Force, I think, fired, but it was ten miles away or something. You don&#39;t hear it.</div><div class="timestamp">16:00</div><div class="transcript-line">You can&#39;t only hear about a quarter mile in the jungle. No artillery, no nothing, just absolute quiet. But I kept talking to uh it wouldn&#39;t have been Lieutenant Baker because he would have been busy.</div><div class="timestamp">16:10</div><div class="transcript-line">It would have been somebody was his kilo or the platoon sergeant.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">16:19</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, I was in the FDC.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">16:27</div><div class="transcript-line">So that was in a gunhole and if it rained it rained on him.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">16:31</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, and there was constant radio chatter, radio traffic with the with the elements of our company. We didn&#39;t have any contact. with Alpha Company because we&#39;re not Alpha Company, but we had constant contact with Delta and there was no fire mission, but we had to be ready because you know we everybody was in the gun holes everybody.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">16:49</div><div class="transcript-line">I assume that they&#39;re moving the guns to keep up with each thing I called.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">16:52</div><div class="transcript-line">And setting deflections as you marched.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">16:54</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Everything uh Our conversation between 8 and 10, I would call back and say, don&#39;t fire, of course. We&#39;re still on it, we&#39;re not allowed to fire, but lay on Alpha 1. And calm, yes sir. And I just knew this is good.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">17:09</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">17:11</div><div class="transcript-line">I know they&#39;re back there just like that. And if I say fire, they&#39;ll fire and Uh eight seconds later, twenty seconds later, depending on how high it goes, I&#39;m gonna get around, land right there. It was from eight to ten constant contact with the uh mortars, constant contact with the uh artillery, but always don&#39;t shoot, don&#39;t shoot.</div><div class="timestamp">17:32</div><div class="transcript-line">Now, at ten o&#39;clock, or by at ten o&#39;clock I I I called back to the mortars and I said, hey, fire Alpha 7 and Bravo 7, because uh this is bad news. I don&#39;t care, you know, fire. I don&#39;t that we&#39;re not sneaking up on them anymore.</div><div class="timestamp">17:50</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s a I didn&#39;t know there&#39;s a thousand of them out there, but I knew there was a lot of them out there.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">17:54</div><div class="transcript-line">And this was subsequent to Alpha&#39;s Contact?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">17:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Yes, about the same, almost instantly with Alpha&#39;s Contact. And the mortars, uh mortars belonged to me I certainly thought that I would call back and say, hey, I don&#39;t give a shit about the rules, fire Alpha 7, fire Bravo 7. And I did something like that.</div><div class="timestamp">18:14</div><div class="transcript-line">No excitement yet, just, okay, all bets are off. Fire Alpha 7, Fire Bravo 7. And nothing happened.</div><div class="timestamp">18:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And I yelled at the uh Lieutenant Durham, get the artillery. Now, between the time of the first round and the time that Alpha Company became non-combat effective. Not because of any I mean the Army doctrine says if you&#39;re ambushed by a group of people that outnumber you, you run away.</div><div class="timestamp">18:42</div><div class="transcript-line">So Alpha the Company didn&#39;t do anything wrong. Alpha Company do what you&#39;re told to do. But by two minutes, five minutes past ten, Alpha Company was gone.</div><div class="timestamp">18:54</div><div class="transcript-line">So there was nobody in Alpha Company to call for mortar fire. Alpha Company never fired their mortars because nobody could call for them No artillery ever fired in support of Alpha Company because the Forward Observer was hit and killed, I think, in the first burst. So I left it up to Lieutenant Durham to talk to the artillerymen to I don&#39;t care.</div><div class="timestamp">19:14</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, d what do you have to have a secret code word? I didn&#39;t say that, but the you know, tell him to shoot for us. And the resolution of the artilleries, which is different from he and I. Resident Lucian Turnbury was by about 15 or 20 minutes.</div><div class="timestamp">19:27</div><div class="transcript-line">My Lieutenant Durham had been able to, I think you know the exact times, but about 15 minutes. My Lieutenant Durham, who I&#39;m screaming at right here is in contact with a captain, somebody in the artillery, who had said, I&#39;ve got orders not to fire for you, but I&#39;m gonna fire anyway. And I think we know who those people are and I think he doesn&#39;t want to talk about it.</div><div class="timestamp">19:48</div><div class="transcript-line">But and artillery captain back there. So now we had artillery firing to our front, but pretty far to our front because Alpha Company is in between us and it was a so now the artillery is pretty well under control. And Pinky&#39;s doing it.</div><div class="timestamp">20:04</div><div class="transcript-line">Now, I still don&#39;t d no mortars are firing. And again, I I&#39;m not mad at them because they&#39;re they&#39;re, you know, I I didn&#39;t uh uh I knew that nobody back there just said well they&#39;re confused It&#39;s just what in the hell is wrong? And nobody told me what was wrong.</div><div class="timestamp">20:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And nobody told me what was wrong. It&#39;s just fire, fire, for Christ&#39;s sake, fire. And no fire, no fire.</div><div class="timestamp">20:29</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I believe Lieutenant Baker came on because he had a special call sign there. I think Lieutenant Bager himself. Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">20:38</div><div class="transcript-line">came on and said I was uh what was I Dauntless Delta Six. He would just call me six. Six this is uh</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">20:46</div><div class="transcript-line">4-6.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">20:48</div><div class="transcript-line">And I knew it was him. And I said, what the hell&#39;s wrong? What the hell&#39;s wrong?</div><div class="timestamp">20:53</div><div class="transcript-line">And he said, they&#39;re not letting me fire. The major&#39;s down here with a pistol. This is what he said.</div><div class="timestamp">20:58</div><div class="transcript-line">The major&#39;s down here with a pistol, he&#39;s not letting me fire. Now, I I I just, you know, b pe we had a lot of dead people. We had within minutes we had so many dead people that we couldn&#39;t you don&#39;t leave anybody there.</div><div class="timestamp">21:13</div><div class="transcript-line">So if everybody that&#39;s alive picks up one dead one, and that&#39;s hard to do, usually it takes two people to carry a dead one. So within minutes, there was no, it&#39;s too late to run away. So it&#39;s just uh, Jesus Christ, fire And then when he said that, that there&#39;s a major here with a pistol, that was incom I mean, that&#39;s incomprehensible.</div><div class="timestamp">21:33</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean what my mortars worked yesterday, talked to you guys this morning, been talking to you for two hours I didn&#39;t say all that, I just said, fire for Christ&#39;s sake, fire. And then after two or three calls of me calling him, And saying, fire. At this time, fire anything, you know, just any way a shot would have killed him.</div><div class="timestamp">21:56</div><div class="transcript-line">Just fire. And and uh then Maeger is off, now I&#39;m talking to somebody else, and it&#39;s just we can&#39;t, sir, we can&#39;t. And finally, I don&#39;t know how long it was, 20, 30 minutes. I just didn&#39;t call for them.</div><div class="timestamp">22:12</div><div class="transcript-line">Well, my radio gets shot. If we found a good radio, we gave it to Lieutenant Durham. So it wasn&#39;t too long and I didn&#39;t have any radio.</div><div class="timestamp">22:21</div><div class="transcript-line">But didn&#39;t make it my battalion commander was right there, I could talk to him. My three platoon leaders were right there, I could talk to them. These guys I don&#39;t think I believed that I couldn&#39;t comprehend what was going on as that&#39;ll lead up, he&#39;ll tell you.</div><div class="timestamp">22:37</div><div class="transcript-line">I couldn&#39;t comprehend what was going on back there. I had called back correctly. I&#39;d talked to the person correctly.</div><div class="timestamp">22:44</div><div class="transcript-line">I was calm at the beginning. I got kind of screwed up later on. And and they weren&#39;t shooting.</div><div class="timestamp">22:50</div><div class="transcript-line">So when it came time, the a radio would get knocked out. Okay, I gotta give up, you know, the the the artillery man would lose his radio. You gotta keep so you bring a radio up to him And then you&#39;re coming back and some other guys, radio operators were getting hit and killed.</div><div class="timestamp">23:06</div><div class="transcript-line">At some point, probably 10. 30, I no longer even tried to call the mortars. Just this is I don&#39;t know why.</div><div class="timestamp">23:16</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I vaguely remember But I remember guys from Recon Platoon. He was right, I don&#39;t know. I remember guys from Recon Platoon coming in very late in the day.</div><div class="timestamp">23:29</div><div class="transcript-line">It wasn&#39;t, you know, the battle started at 10, on the on the drop of ten. The enemy commander said that. I saw it on my watch.</div><div class="timestamp">23:36</div><div class="transcript-line">The enemy the firing started at ten, two plus two or three seconds The firing ended at twelve according to the enemy commander. All I know my watch was blow blown off, so I don&#39;t know what time it was. After half hour I stopped calling them.</div><div class="timestamp">23:53</div><div class="transcript-line">After half hour I wasn&#39;t, I just, they&#39;re not shooting. Certainly be nice if they were shooting. And then I was unconscious and evacuated, so I never knew, never knew, and then he&#39;ll fill in, but and until I met Lieutenant Bager Between</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">24:17</div><div class="transcript-line">Probably at Reno.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">24:18</div><div class="transcript-line">At Reno four or six years ago. Yeah. And he came up to me and and I I thought I recognized him, but I don&#39;t want to make a mistake with these guys.</div><div class="timestamp">24:27</div><div class="transcript-line">And he said, Sir, I&#39;m Lieutenant Maker. And he started to cry. He said, I&#39;m so sorry.</div><div class="timestamp">24:33</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m so sorry. That I that I didn&#39;t shoot. And he was sobbing.</div><div class="timestamp">24:38</div><div class="transcript-line">I was sobbing. I&#39;m not making fun of anybody. This is what, 35 years later And he has spent a pretty goddamn difficult 35 years.</div><div class="timestamp">24:48</div><div class="transcript-line">When I knew him, smart, bright, Probably a college graduate because the college graduate went in the recon went in scout platoon went in the mortar platoon because they knew how to do figures. Smart, bright, good kid. And then he had, from his words to me at Reno, he had had not a good life.</div><div class="timestamp">25:08</div><div class="transcript-line">And then he died</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">25:11</div><div class="transcript-line">He was died in June of 06, he was murdered.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div clas