 started and and the shooting starts w and you can hear it in the distance and it&#39;s like it it&#39;s like all firefight start it&#39;s an occasional but but you know and all of a sudden it it reaches a crescendo at the crescendo time then the um there was an RTO on the phone, I don&#39;t know who it was, and he was calling for information or something, and everything starts to to to kind kind of focus at one point. You know, there&#39;s all kinds of chaos and and you know triple the chaos for for those out there and we&#39;re waiting you know for the call we&#39;re waiting to be able to shoot and I didn&#39;t know why and I&#39;m the guy that had to know why you know and I didn&#39;t know why And I think Megger was maybe maybe busy, I&#39;m just guessing, trying to get that permission. But I think the permission was, even if he got it from the major, I think the major was being directed and And and Colonel Welsh will tell me if I&#39;m wrong.</div><div class="timestamp">27:11</div><div class="transcript-line">Maybe we would be indirected from a higher level, perhaps.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">27:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t know why. All I know is I said shoot and he didn&#39;t shoot.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">27:19</div><div class="transcript-line">So you still don&#39;t know why?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">27:20</div><div class="transcript-line">Well, but I recall a passage in in in David&#39;s book. We can read the book now. Something like division was afraid of of errors being made by mortars with people maneuvering in the jungle.</div><div class="timestamp">27:35</div><div class="transcript-line">And I&#39;ll tell you, after a while, after the battle has had continued for a while, I mean Those guys had nothing to lose if we dropped a few rounds on them by mistake. Exactly. Because they were in among our wounded, you know, and they were in among everybody.</div><div class="timestamp">27:50</div><div class="transcript-line">And so At that point, there was nothing to lose. You know, people were displacing everywhere. You know, so and and you know, that&#39;s I mean that&#39;s my most that was my foremost worry is beating the FTC in words was was killing my own guys, you know.</div><div class="timestamp">28:03</div><div class="transcript-line">You didn&#39;t shoot at all, Led. No, no, but what I&#39;m saying, that was a I was always a worry, but if we&#39;d if if we Because division according to the book division was saying that you know that&#39;s that&#39;s why we&#39;re hesitant, you know, but what if you have to lose? I mean and and then we&#39;d already done so well on this sixteenth, you know.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">28:18</div><div class="transcript-line">So it was showed When I got up in the hospital later, that was one of my many questions. And and uh that I think I&#39;ve told you that the circumstances of my recovery in the hospital where I was so enraged, I couldn&#39;t even find out who&#39;d been dead, who&#39;d been alive. I vaguely remember it&#39;s you know, you you see some guy get hit in the chest eight times.</div><div class="timestamp">28:44</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay, he&#39;s dead, I should write to his mother, but maybe he&#39;s not dead, maybe it&#39;s somebody else, and I didn&#39;t have the mother&#39;s address as it didn&#39;t have anything I did have a question about wonder why the, it wasn&#39;t like that. Wonder why the mortars didn&#39;t fire. My beautiful mortars.</div><div class="timestamp">28:57</div><div class="transcript-line">It didn&#39;t, not a word. And after that first, you talk about it in your book, Frillow, after that initial month or two of unbelief that this cannot have happened. The question of, you know, the question of who&#39;s dead, who&#39;s alive, that&#39;s important.</div><div class="timestamp">29:16</div><div class="transcript-line">The question of, gee, how come the motors didn&#39;t fire? That kind of fell down the bottom. And I did not think anything about, I wouldn&#39;t say that, I thought about it every day, but I never uh, I wouldn&#39;t have called uh Sergeant Alban up and said uh Hey, you&#39;re my brother 17th, how come you didn&#39;t shoot?</div><div class="timestamp">29:33</div><div class="transcript-line">We we wouldn&#39;t have uh he probably wouldn&#39;t want to call I don&#39;t want to say for him, but he would have wouldn&#39;t want to call me because it&#39;s embarrassing. Hey Lieutenant, how come you didn&#39;t ask for fire? What&#39;s the matter with you</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">29:43</div><div class="transcript-line">With all due respect, I didn&#39;t even know you were alive.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">29:45</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, well I didn&#39;t know it was you know yeah it was just uh uh so that for that two months that you talk about, it&#39;s just God almighty, mortars didn&#39;t fire yet, but man, there&#39;s a bunch of other stuff And then the way I survived, everybody picked a way to survive, was after that two months of not being able to accomplish anything, and I had another bad month, and I just figured, man, it didn&#39;t happen. Didn&#39;t happen. And it wasn&#39;t until General Cavasso went to the war col uh Colonel Cavasso in command of the first E18th, yes.</div><div class="timestamp">30:17</div><div class="transcript-line">Super commander. And I I worked from a couple of times later on. He&#39;s a retired four-star now.</div><div class="timestamp">30:22</div><div class="transcript-line">Colonel Cavasso went to the War College, and when he was there, he worked on a project I think called HERO, H-E-R-O, and that acronym means something, humanity in battle or some crap. And what he was studying was defeats. And you list what what do you learn from a defeat?</div><div class="timestamp">30:40</div><div class="transcript-line">Because normally we don&#39;t read much about defeats, as we know. And he called me one time and said, would you be willing to talk to me for Project Hero? And then he flew to wherever I was, not where I was, Florida Ranger Camp, I think.</div><div class="timestamp">30:55</div><div class="transcript-line">and had a questionnaire and in that questionnaire was uh how was your mortar fire? And I remember sitting there with him in an old barracks at uh probably three years later, four years later, at uh the Florida Ranger Camp, Egland Air Force Base. As he was going down through the list, it was just terrible question after terrible question.</div><div class="timestamp">31:16</div><div class="transcript-line">Like uh, you know, where was he? And my answers are this one. So we didn&#39;t do that.</div><div class="timestamp">31:22</div><div class="transcript-line">We didn&#39;t do this. Nobody did this. I didn&#39;t hear any airplanes.</div><div class="timestamp">31:25</div><div class="transcript-line">I didn&#39;t hear any helicopters. And then we came to that question about, and how is your mortar fire? I just lost it.</div><div class="timestamp">31:32</div><div class="transcript-line">I said there was no mortar fire. And okay, so I thought about mortars for two minutes, three years later. And then 35 years later I see at some other reunions we didn&#39;t have any reunions for 30 years?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">31:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, because there was no contact basically.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">31:51</div><div class="transcript-line">No contact. No, we would have avoided. Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">31:54</div><div class="transcript-line">Like the same thing. I w I would have been afraid that he would have said, well, you dumb shit,