s two, three miles. They were mine. Just unbelievable.</div><div class="timestamp">33:27</div><div class="transcript-line">That uh they didn&#39;t shoot when I asked them to. It wasn&#39;t that I ordered them to. That&#39;s not how you do it.</div><div class="timestamp">33:32</div><div class="transcript-line">You just say, hey, fire E37. And uh It was equ it was frustrating for, I mean obviously more frustrating for</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">33:39</div><div class="transcript-line">Lieutenant Welch, Colonel Welch, than it was for us, but we were extremely frustrated. And the frustration frustration carried over you know, subsequent to the battle and that I don&#39;t recall anybody talking about it anymore. You know, why there weren&#39;t there there weren&#39;t that many guys left to come up and ask us how come you guys didn&#39;t fire.</div><div class="timestamp">33:58</div><div class="transcript-line">And the only response we could have given them is because we didn&#39;t we weren&#39;t allowed to fire, you know. And but nobody did that. You know, I think that everybody just wanted to put it out of their mind.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">34:06</div><div class="transcript-line">And they only asked me that one time when General Cabasso asked.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">34:09</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah And uh in a in a subsequent in uh uh subsequent to the on the on the eighteenth, uh uh Captain Grosso, later our company commander, did one-to-one interviews in the in the mess hall in a secluded area somewhat like this and that question never came up of me. I mean he did it with all the people. There&#39;s like maybe thirty guys left in the company.</div><div class="timestamp">34:29</div><div class="transcript-line">Most of those guys were border guys. And he asked you know he did he asked All kinds of questions, but that one I don&#39;t believe ever came up. And if it did, it&#39;s in the transcript somewhere, but I don&#39;t recall him saying, how come the borders didn&#39;t fire.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">34:42</div><div class="transcript-line">And now Major is dead.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">34:45</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">34:45</div><div class="transcript-line">But then the answer comes out later, you know, apparently that we think we know &#39;cause we or I think I know because I read it.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">34:52</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">34:52</div><div class="transcript-line">And it certainly matches everything else that was going on that day.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">34:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">34:56</div><div class="transcript-line">They fired the 16th, fired really good. The fact that you fired all the way out and then they fired us all the way back. Mortars are wonderful</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">35:07</div><div class="transcript-line">Mortars In the hands of the right people.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">35:11</div><div class="transcript-line">They were good. They were very good. They were very good and they&#39;re very proud. I don&#39;t know if they&#39;d been a mortar platoon before even. I think we might have just drawn, you know, count off by fours and everybody&#39;s a four year on the mortar platoon.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">35:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I fortunately had mortar training. Yeah, well, yeah. There was other guys there that had more training. Some some were just put in there because they needed bodies, you know.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">35:31</div><div class="transcript-line">But they had to carry you carry a mortar round. We didn&#39;t have any trucks or anything. So they carried the mortar rounds in addition to all the other stuff. They carry these big heavy mortar rounds.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">35:41</div><div class="transcript-line">Is there anything you&#39;d like to add about the events of the day?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">35:47</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s a really broad question of Well I mean from the actual attack</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">35:53</div><div class="transcript-line">The battle itself.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">35:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Um one of my everlasting memories is somebody Midway through frantically calling back for a back azimuth, coming over the radio in the gunpit. And this guy was just desperate. So we gave him a back azimuth which is the reverse of your you know y y it&#39;s it&#39;s just a you know turn around it&#39;s a turnaround.</div><div class="timestamp">36:16</div><div class="transcript-line">The azimuth was directly south, the back azimuth is directly north. And the very the very question give us a back azimuth It shows the predicament they were in because I mean there was panic there at that time. Most guys know that if you go out on a on a you know uh three sixty the back azimuth is a one eighty.</div><div class="timestamp">36:34</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s just a you know reverse. So that and then after we weren&#39;t allowed to fire I got out of the gun hole and I was walking around and I went out to the perimeter and this must have been maybe I have no clue what time. Before twelve, but after 1030.</div><div class="timestamp">36:47</div><div class="transcript-line">And here comes a a American soldier running with nothing on. I mean a pair of pants maybe I guess, but no web gear, helmet, nada.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">36:57</div><div class="transcript-line">They&#39;re not running away. That&#39;s the what&#39;s what the army teaches you. When you&#39;re caught in the kills over an ambush, you run away.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">37:03</div><div class="transcript-line">And this guy had nothing. And I s then it dawned it dawned on me then it must be horrific there because I&#39;d you know been in Vietnam at that time for five or six months and been through a number of firefights either in the border pit or whatever and I&#39;d never seen that occur. You know so that&#39;s like a memory and then go actually going out there and finding Sergeant Barrow there that&#39;s</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">37:23</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s something that uh when you look at this guy, I I&#39;m uh it&#39;s not bull I&#39;m not you don&#39;t bullshit anybody anymore. But I was out there because I was told to go out there. I had 150 guys, 142 of us, I had mortars in it.</div><div class="timestamp">37:36</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s not bad. You know, you go out there, that&#39;s what you&#39;re over there for. You&#39;re out there to do to fight.</div><div class="timestamp">37:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Now, think of these guys. He&#39;s sitting back where he&#39;s safe. Uh there&#39;s uh there were more people back there where he was than there was with me.</div><div class="timestamp">37:51</div><div class="transcript-line">Many more. Uh he&#39;s got more guns than you could possibly carry in ten trucks, he&#39;s got plenty of ammunition, he&#39;s got water, he&#39;s got Medevac helicopters, what do you mean that stuff? And he, he&#39;s in the mortar platoon.</div><div class="timestamp">38:05</div><div class="transcript-line">His job is to stay back there. Nobody&#39;s nobody&#39;s gonna tell him to go forward. His job is to stay back there.</div><div class="timestamp">38:12</div><div class="transcript-line">And he and dozens of other men with him. without orders, because nobody in the goddamn army said, go help Welch or go help nobody until Major Hollander did, and he said, go help them, and they got killed. And that was later.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">38:27</div><div class="transcript-line">Yes.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">38:28</div><div class="transcript-line">But somehow he, Tom Hinger, uh I I don&#39;t want to get the wrong names. But back there in the in the safety of there, they&#39;re in a gun pit. It&#39;s a hole in the ground.</div><div class="timestamp">38:37</div><div class="transcript-line">They&#39;re perfectly safe. They got ice water The chow trucks probably coming in? Because it uh we were less than a click away, but uh these guys led by Recon, Recon sergeant, because the Recon platoon leader decided not to go, a bunch of sergeants and privates pick up From where they&#39;re safe, drinking ice water, and walk into where they know there&#39;s just been a total massacre And and they just uh I d I&#39;d like to say that I remember them showing up.</div><div class="timestamp">39:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t I remember the the scouts showing up, but I don&#39;t really remember that just kind of foggy. But but for these guys, uh It&#39;s an amazing I I think about that often what they uh I didn&#39;t have any choice. I was out there, I had a choice, I could run away or not run away, and I decided not to run away.</div><div class="timestamp">39:32</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s easy But for these guys to be, it&#39;s him and the other 20, 25 guys that were within that first burst that came out to get us. Guys who don&#39;t have to go out. Their job is to stay back there, which nice and clean, you know, far to the rear.</div><div class="timestamp">39:49</div><div class="transcript-line">And they Yeah, the fighting, a lot of the fighting was over by then, but they were still, as I know now, there were 200 men that worked for Captain Lom, whose job was just to hang around and pick off And I think that&#39;s how they got uh Major Hollander. That&#39;s how they got Major Hollander.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">40:05</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">40:05</div><div class="transcript-line">The fight was over. But did these guys, him, happen happens to be him. I I I wouldn&#39;t know that because I didn&#39;t see him, but I believe that he did.</div><div class="timestamp">40:16</div><div class="transcript-line">left, left the safety of the NDP and walked forward. They didn&#39;t know how far. You could follow the trail, I guess, a close and stuff, but then But uh it&#39;s very reassuring for me to come back with what I remember and have him not say, wait a minute, it wasn&#39;t like that at all.</div><div class="timestamp">40:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Because the memory of what happened is so incredibly stupid. Just horrible after horrible after horrible that that couldn&#39;t have happened</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">40:51</div><div class="transcript-line">But for me to say, uh, geez, I called and nobody ever shot there was no question that I mean of the people that went there you know out there on the I don&#39;t know what you want to call it, it wasn&#39;t a rescue mission, but Relief mission or whatever, there was no question of of not going. It&#39;s just a question if you were available and you could go. And I mean I was expendable because nobody was going to fire the mortars then anyway, you know.</div><div class="timestamp">41:19</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean why not, you know? And there was guys out there that you know, needed help and if they were going to be evacuated somebody&#39;s got to carry them out because triple canopy wouldn&#39;t allow for many helicopters to come in and and evacuate &#39;em. So it was just, you know, kind of like common sense.</div><div class="timestamp">41:33</div><div class="transcript-line">You better get out there as quickly as possible and get as many people back in the case. And call it common sense. It&#39;s not common it&#39;s not common sense to go walking into something where you know that I was talking about you know the common sense angle of of how do you get people out of triple canopy canopy jungle without having to carry &#39;em because uh usually you know helicopter those guys would ch set those choppers down anywhere.</div><div class="timestamp">41:53</div><div class="transcript-line">But they they have to have a place And I think we had to make the place with chainsaws and that, and it was a very limited space. So as a result, I think I was telling you yesterday, some guys were left out there overnight that overnight that were dead, you know. And covered with artillery barrage, you know, all night long and that.</div><div class="timestamp">42:10</div><div class="transcript-line">But, you know, that was another thing. So how do we get the guys out? How do we get everybody out, alive or dead?</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">42:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Did you agree with the decision to leave them overnight?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">42:19</div><div class="transcript-line">I had no part in that decision. I didn&#39;t know that anybody was left until I read the book. And if If it would have been, you know, my call, I I I I mean I would have tried, but we tried to carry, I carried out as many guys as we could back to a central Bad word depository, you know, and then as many as could be evacuated by helicopter were, but I wasn&#39;t part of the logistics, so It&#39;s a written rule.</div><div class="timestamp">42:47</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Yeah. Do not leave anybody behind.</div><div class="timestamp">42:49</div><div class="transcript-line">And in that case it had to be extraordinary that those those Those KIA got left behind.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">42:55</div><div class="transcript-line">And it it I I I did not know any but one was left behind that day, of course. I&#39;m not sure how I got out. I&#39;ve had a two well now that the book has been written there&#39;s a dozen people claim to have carried me out.</div><div class="timestamp">43:07</div><div class="transcript-line">But I I don&#39;t think it&#39;s all anyway. I don&#39;t know what happened that night. Later on when I find out that the brigade commander ordered them and although that looks fine in a report if you do the kind of stuff that I&#39;ve spent my life doing in the Army, wait a minute.</div><div class="timestamp">43:25</div><div class="transcript-line">The brigade commander didn&#39;t get to 2 o&#39;clock. The sun didn&#39;t go down until 8 30. That was a full moon.</div><div class="timestamp">43:33</div><div class="transcript-line">How can he claim that he had to leave them behind because it was too dark? Bullshit. Okay.</div><div class="timestamp">43:38</div><div class="transcript-line">Bullshit Uh that now d does anybody know one of those reports? Did they look it up like I did to find out it&#39;s a full moon? Uh kind of a complicated table you have to find to get there.</div><div class="timestamp">43:48</div><div class="transcript-line">And it didn&#39;t get dark. Uh When I read later on, uh or you don&#39;t read until lately, but when I would see things later on that uh bodies were left behind and then usually parentheses except for the battalion commander who was taken out. What the hell is that?</div><div class="timestamp">44:04</div><div class="transcript-line">When I read that, I just couldn&#39;t believe it. And it was one of those things that I&#39;m uh these guys didn&#39;t leave anybody behind. No nobody that I know, not in my army, don&#39;t leave people behind.</div><div class="timestamp">44:15</div><div class="transcript-line">And when I went over to Vietnam the first time with the man who wrote the book and met Captain Lom. Captain Lom is the man who lived, lives in the same little house that he&#39;s lived, his family&#39;s lived in for a thousand years. dirt floor, got a concrete floor now because he&#39;s very famous because he was in the book.</div><div class="timestamp">44:31</div><div class="transcript-line">He&#39;s got electricity, got a little rice cooker, got a TV. Captain Lom&#39;s job was to keep he owned that base camp, uh s six kilometers by four kilometers. He owned that.</div><div class="timestamp">44:43</div><div class="transcript-line">And his job was to, when there was nobody around, go up to Cambodia and get rice and bring it down, maintain a doctor place, maintain a dead place. maintainer, sewing machine place, uh weapons. They made all this underground and bunkers left over left behind by the Viet Minh and the French.</div><div class="timestamp">45:00</div><div class="transcript-line">Now when I went to see him, and we were on the battlefield, and I&#39;m talking with Colonel Triet about the battle because Colonel Triet ran the battle, but Captain Lum is with us because he&#39;s the guy that was there. He stays there all the time And uh through that conversation, if I told you before, I don&#39;t, but through when Captain Lam stopped and looked at me in some English, some Vietnamese. But my Vietnamese came back enough so it was just like we were good old friends talking.</div><div class="timestamp">45:26</div><div class="transcript-line">And he put his hand in my chest and he said, he Colonel, they were very correct. They called me Colonel Welch. I called him Colonel Triet, Captain Lam No doubt about it.</div><div class="timestamp">45:35</div><div class="transcript-line">A not my friend, friend&#39;s not the right word, but my warrior comrade or something. We&#39;re we&#39;re we&#39;re friends. And uh little Captain Lobb, little tiny guy, pokes me in the chest.</div><div class="timestamp">45:47</div><div class="transcript-line">You&#39;re not a very good soldier, Colonel Weltson. And that&#39;s not a good thing to say to me. That&#39;s uh just a you can&#39;t I can&#39;t think of anything to say to me any words.</div><div class="timestamp">45:55</div><div class="transcript-line">You&#39;re not a good soldier And I said, what are you talking about? I thought maybe I misunderstood. He said, you left your dead here.</div><div class="timestamp">46:01</div><div class="transcript-line">Now, when Captain Lum says that, yeah, Captain Lum. Now his job is to, after the battle&#39;s over, he goes around the battlefield, he ignores the Americans, doesn&#39;t bury them, doesn&#39;t do anything with them, doesn&#39;t want to touch them. They&#39;re like space aliens.</div><div class="timestamp">46:16</div><div class="transcript-line">Captain Alam goes around, picks up the wounded guys, brings them to the doctor place, the dead guys bring them to the dead place, sends the numbers back. That&#39;s his job. When he said to me, you&#39;re not a good soldier, you left your dead here And I it really made me mad.</div><div class="timestamp">46:31</div><div class="transcript-line">And I want to say, hey, don&#39;t blame me. I was uh, and I I did you don&#39;t say don&#39;t blame me, but that was what I want to say. And and it&#39;s but I just yes we did.</div><div class="timestamp">46:43</div><div class="transcript-line">We did. He said you left them here for five days. Now I had read the reports that said it was only overnight.</div><div class="timestamp">46:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Now what Captain Lung told me that he every day of his life he gets out walks around. Sometimes there&#39;s Americans there, sometimes there&#39;s not. He walks around.</div><div class="timestamp">46:58</div><div class="transcript-line">He had to walk about a half mile to go to a good well that was the far end of that draw. And that uh that night while he came out on the battlefield to recover his own dead and wounded, because Colonel Triet left at 12 o&#39;clock. When Captain Lom came out that night, he found what he called a stack of bodies.</div><div class="timestamp">47:19</div><div class="transcript-line">He said they were right there. And he said they were like firewood. This is what he&#39;s telling me just four years ago.</div><div class="timestamp">47:25</div><div class="transcript-line">And I well We shouldn&#39;t have left him there. Very bad soldier, very bad soldier. And stayed out here for five days.</div><div class="timestamp">47:33</div><div class="transcript-line">And I said, oh, five days. I thought Everybody came out the next day. And he said most of them were recovered the next day at as soon as it got light.</div><div class="timestamp">47:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Most of them were recovered. But I&#39;ll take you to where there was a body and it stayed there for five days. And then David Moranis was hoping that that would be the body of one of our MIA, because we uh we, I say we, it was Alpha Company, but it&#39;s all the world together.</div><div class="timestamp">48:00</div><div class="transcript-line">We have we still have two MIA. And I went back to Vietnam in 73 to look for MIA.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">48:07</div><div class="transcript-line">That was Hargrove and Fitzgerald.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">48:08</div><div class="transcript-line">Hargrove and Fitzgerald, two tall black guys. that I saw running away as they should, two tall black guys running through the woods with with no shirts. run on the wrong direction.</div><div class="timestamp">48:21</div><div class="transcript-line">They ran that way and what you should have done was come back to me or gone that way, but it they went the right. But anyway, uh David Moratus was talking he&#39;s writing a book he says this will be cool you know technique maybe we can find the missing in action and uh we went back to it it made me think that Captain Lom was telling the truth because he said it&#39;s right over here that where the battlefield is has all been made over into rubber. The Russians did that in their five-year plan.</div><div class="timestamp">48:47</div><div class="transcript-line">And that rubber grew up and then this is the second crop of rubber. So everything&#39;s different. But we walked over to a place on the other side of the drawer and he said that big tree, and you could still see the hulk of a big tree, he was right at the bottom of that big tree.</div><div class="timestamp">49:00</div><div class="transcript-line">It kind of gross I wouldn&#39;t want You know, once you quote it, the uh he said the pigs come out and eat on him at night. I don&#39;t know if that&#39;s true or not. That may be just a Vietnamese phrase.</div><div class="timestamp">49:15</div><div class="transcript-line">He said he&#39;d been all blown up like a bomb. I don&#39;t know. Now David Moranis wanted it to be somebody, and he was trying to angle it so he&#39;d be able to get, and David Moranis is my dear friend.</div><div class="timestamp">49:29</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m not saying he&#39;s doing anything wrong, but his job is to write a book. and he wants to find out something neat about this. So we go back there and he starts to talk to the guy about, you know, how big it was and I said, I said to Captain Lum, uh this man you found here.</div><div class="timestamp">49:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Did he have uh white skin or black skin? And the Vietnamese are very aware they don&#39;t like black people, they don&#39;t like Cambodians, they don&#39;t like Koreans, they don&#39;t they&#39;re very uh uh what I don&#39;t know, nationalistic or something. And he and he uh I said to the you know white skin and black skin.</div><div class="timestamp">49:59</div><div class="transcript-line">He said, no black, no black, white, white, white, like you So that disappointed David Randis. That disappointed David Randis that it wasn&#39;t the uh missing guy. But the point is, incredibly The United States Army decided to leave 25, 35 bodies there, stacked like firewood.</div><div class="timestamp">50:20</div><div class="transcript-line">And they they claim that they fired artillery on them all night. Well, artillery is no good unless you get somebody there to watch it. &#39;Cause I don&#39;t know how it happens, but the guns barrels droop a little bit, so you shoot, boom.</div><div class="timestamp">50:33</div><div class="transcript-line">Next time you shoot goes there, boom. Now, if you look on the Vietnam Wall All, or almost all, of our dead from that day died of shrapnel wounds. Now I believe that during the night our artillery hit some of our dead men.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">50:58</div><div class="transcript-line">And I think I told you uh yesterday, Carrie, that according to the book, that was something that bothered me that that The next day they went out and they found several that weren&#39;t stacked, and two of that several had no rigor mortise set in at that point. And I mentioned that to Joe Devine And he said, get over it. But that&#39;s And and that&#39;s nobody&#39;s fault.</div><div class="timestamp">51:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean we just didn&#39;t the guys didn&#39;t find everybody that was wounded because people were, you know, moving about. And I one of my foremost memories is about two years ago I got a an email from a the sister of an un I&#39;m not going to mention the the guy&#39;s name because he was killed, but the sister writing on behalf of of one of the men in in in our company that was killed. And this is thirty-five years later, thirty-four years later, and this sister was writing on to me on behalf of the mother wanting to know if the son had been evacuated.</div><div class="timestamp">51:58</div><div class="transcript-line">that day or that that day or had he laid on the battlefield overnight and I said to the best of my recollection he had been evacuated and I didn&#39;t know that but I mean as long as this is, you know that was the best way to handle it. Because here&#39;s a mother thirty-five years later wanting to know if her deceased son had been evacuated or not. And for the all for her best interest.</div><div class="timestamp">52:25</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean I didn&#39;t tell the truth there. I didn&#39;t lie, but I didn&#39;t know, so I said, absolutely. You know I remember because I did carry him out.</div><div class="timestamp">52:32</div><div class="transcript-line">I had carried him out.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">52:33</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s just hard to comprehend out.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">52:34</div><div class="transcript-line">I had carried him out and and you know, two men to a stretcher to carry out one guy With a little extra effort, we could have carried out a lot of those guys that were stacked. You know, I I believe. But maybe, you know, again, I&#39;m on the low end of the of the of the f uh the food chain so to speak.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">52:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Maybe there was overriding circumstances and I always want to believe the best of the kind of kind of you know I&#39;m kinda tired of trying to believe I ca