 I&#39;ve said more than I should uh It&#39;s it&#39;s always it&#39;s always uh 40 years too late.</div><div class="timestamp">01:09:59</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, 40 years too late.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:10:02</div><div class="transcript-line">David Maranus&#39;s book, Maranus? Yeah, there&#39;s a way to pronounce it I always forget. in 2003 in October, just after the book was released, and did a question and answer thing.</div><div class="timestamp">01:10:19</div><div class="transcript-line">And I I was there and I met him and I I talked to the guy on the phone and did emails with him, but I never met him. just the nicest guy and I at the very end I I felt I mean I didn&#39;t want to I didn&#39;t tell anybody I was part of that or anything I just stood up and said that from my perspective when the when David wrote the book he had no agenda He was writing a a history, you know, a multifaceted history, and I trusted him completely. After I talked to him for about 10 minutes on the phone, I could get a read on him.</div><div class="timestamp">01:10:50</div><div class="transcript-line">He was just, uh I have nothing good things to say about him and the book, and that&#39;s the way I feel now. The only thing that I regret is that both in well, in in the book, uh I mean and and David Merinus and I get along very well and everything, and I I don&#39;t have any I mean, he wrote the book, it&#39;s his book. But I think from my perspective that Pinky Durham could have gotten more.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:11:16</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s that&#39;s a funny disconnect in there. I don&#39;t understand how he could have had more I mean here&#39;s a guy that wins a congressional.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:11:22</div><div class="transcript-line">We certainly said enough about him. Yeah, he he wins a congressional medal of honor. Maybe because he wasn&#39;t a black lion, but who cares?</div><div class="timestamp">01:11:28</div><div class="transcript-line">He was he was with us and he was defending himself and us. So that and then of course You turn to uh Two Days in October by Robbie Kenner and I&#39;ve I&#39;ve contacted him and you know talked to him. Um that was his piece, obviously, but there&#39;s not a mention In that 90 minute documentary of the year.</div><div class="timestamp">01:11:48</div><div class="transcript-line">Very powerful documentary, but very powerful, but not mentioning Pinky Durham at all.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:11:53</div><div class="transcript-line">Durham&#39;s family is not here now, and they would have been last year, last year, last year. Pinky Durham&#39;s brother who was in the army And Pinky Durham s sister. Uh Pinky wasn&#39;t married. Pinky had a bunch of girlfriends we found.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:06</div><div class="transcript-line">But I don&#39;t know why the You know, it&#39;s it it the the the film sound like that we were I mean, at least when I watched it, I watched it several times. It it seemed to me like they portrayed Delta and Alpha Company as victims. you know, uh rather as defenseless victims I got the feeling, you know, that we did it and we just stood there and took fire, you know. But I mean I&#39;m telling you that there were There were countless acts that day that&#39;ll go un you know I mean un untold because nobody survived. But if you watch the film, you would say that we never maybe squeezed off around</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:46</div><div class="transcript-line">Well some of the guys said that. And that was the quote they picked out to use, you know, that I didn&#39;t shoot very much.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:52</div><div class="transcript-line">But damn. I mean, I&#39;ve always said that I think in that thing that uncommon valor was common that day. And I&#39;ll go to my grave thinking that, but it&#39;ll be unreported for the most part because There&#39;s only a handful of guys that lived in the middle.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:07</div><div class="transcript-line">You know that the uh movie They Were Soldiers Once in Young. That battalion commander survived and went on to be a four-star general. That man that was we had a news we had a photographer with us named Gilbert.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:23</div><div class="transcript-line">He was killed right away. Who was killed? Well, the photographer that was with the we were soldiers once in young grew up to write a Pillitzer Prize and be a famous guy.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:34</div><div class="transcript-line">And I I would I don&#39;t say I won&#39;t give anything, it&#39;s too late to say, but my guys deserve that Two of our guys have been disinterred because when their bodies were sent home, the bodies were just sent home. He died. They didn&#39;t have wives, didn&#39;t have families, they had mothers.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:51</div><div class="transcript-line">They all had mothers And uh, but now we&#39;re in touch with the people. And uh I wish you could have been there the day that uh chiseling Tizial. Tizio radio operator, that his his little sister met my two radio operators because they knew Tizio, radio, two both named Scott.</div><div class="timestamp">01:14:15</div><div class="transcript-line">And uh I saw the three of them talking over there and they&#39;re crying and I went over to of course everybody&#39;s crying. The get together in Las Vegas is different from here. Yeah, yeah, it&#39;s it&#39;s uh it&#39;s a hard it&#39;s uh but I say what&#39;s can I help?</div><div class="timestamp">01:14:33</div><div class="transcript-line">What&#39;s the matter? And and she said, and my two guys, two Scots, Scott up and Scott down, are saying that uh they just told her That Tizio said, if I die, and I&#39;m not going to die, none of us are going to die, but Tizio had told them, and they were relating to his sister, that if I die, I want to be buried at Arlington. And none of our guys are buried at Arlington.</div><div class="timestamp">01:14:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Because the guys who die, they just get sent home. No no no bronze star, no purple heart even. So in in her case So sitting at that table, we agreed that we would do what we could to get him buried in Arlington where he told his buddies he wanted to be.</div><div class="timestamp">01:15:12</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I wrote a couple letters and I finally got a letter back from somebody that said Uh uh y you can only be reinterred in Arlington if you get a note from the President of the United States, some general. And I wrote a letter back to that general saying, Well you damn fool? Call the President of the United States And General Shelton got me quieter down to write a letter correctly.</div><div class="timestamp">01:15:33</div><div class="transcript-line">And we got the note. It doesn&#39;t have to be signed by the president anymore, but we got the note signed by the president. And he was dug up from this little family plot in New downtown New York City.</div><div class="timestamp">01:15:43</div><div class="transcript-line">And we re-interred him, I&#39;ve learned there&#39;s the name And we had uh David Moranis was there. Twenty guys were there. Ed Burke was there with a uh have you seen picture of this?</div><div class="timestamp">01:15:53</div><div class="transcript-line">He well he was reinterred there. Now, that&#39;s why I say it&#39;s 35 years too late You know, when these guys came home, when Pinky Durham came home, his father went crazy. His mother died a couple months later.</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:05</div><div class="transcript-line">Father died a couple years later. Uh Pinky Durham came home without the metal bottom. He just came home dead.</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:11</div><div class="transcript-line">It was a year or so later before the uh I don&#39;t think his mother and father were still alive when they presented Pinky the metal bone. Uh this guy Tusio came home. His mother and father died within a couple of months.</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:25</div><div class="transcript-line">So it&#39;s it&#39;s 35, 40 years too late, but it&#39;s uh It&#39;s been very good. Your last chapter. Things are so much better.</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:39</div><div class="transcript-line">They can&#39;t be made right. And you see</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:45</div><div class="transcript-line">The good thing is for me that&#39;s in the public domain now. Yeah. You know, and for all those years I never, there was no need to talk about it because You know, like I told you yesterday, how do you go up and say, oh I was in this battle and we got a lot of guys killed?</div><div class="timestamp">01:16:57</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean that just doesn&#39;t work, you know. So now that it&#39;s in the public domain, if people ask, you can tell them. But even if they don&#39;t ask, people can read about it in the book And they can understand.</div><div class="timestamp">01:17:14</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s very powerful. Yeah, if it&#39;s powerful Somebody thought it was good and it created it it it it caused people that we don&#39;t even know about, you know, to talk. And yeah, in some way it f it makes me feel that those guys got there justice because they didn&#39;t die unknown like so many other there&#39;s there&#39;s a hundred of those battles in Vietnam.</div><div class="timestamp">01:17:32</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean there&#39;s you know I mean not maybe with the intensity and not with all the political juxtaposition and the president here and Wisconsin here. There&#39;s that&#39;s just one day of three sixty five that every soldier served in Vietnam, particularly infantry guys. And now that is out there for people to either read or see or evaluate and that&#39;s a good thing to me.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:17:54</div><div class="transcript-line">It seems like those publications, the reunions, it seems like it&#39;s It&#39;s therapeutic.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:17:59</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, I i it is. I I don&#39;t I don&#39;t understand therapeutic and that other word closure or something. People you have closure?</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:04</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t know. But the uh the the thing that uh you don&#39;t celebrate a losing season. I see these, I really envy these World War II veterans.</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:13</div><div class="transcript-line">Who can show you, whether you see the maps, you can buy a map. You don&#39;t want to see a map of what I did in Vietnam. Went over here, went over here, finally got shot here, got the hospital, came back, went around, got shot, came back, finally went out, went to Japan.</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:24</div><div class="transcript-line">What the hell kind of a map is that? Well these these world I really envy these world wars. I wish I was, you know, born back in the twenty or something.</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:32</div><div class="transcript-line">That the uh uh Iraq is doing, I don&#39;t want to say Iraq, doing the same thing, where they had such a clear-cut thing and they won. You don&#39;t celebrate a losing season. No And now it&#39;s a losing season, yeah, but that&#39;s not what we talk about.</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:49</div><div class="transcript-line">We were winning. When we left, we were winning. that the the enemy colonel said, why&#39;d you leave?</div><div class="timestamp">01:18:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Well we were uh you know Tet uh in that battle he lost some people, he lost so many people. He didn&#39;t lose as many as the report said, 120 or something.</div><div class="timestamp">01:19:04</div><div class="transcript-line">But he said he lost a company. That would be 60 or 80 pounds. But for him to lose a company, that meant that he, what he was supposed to do was pass through, link up with the rest of the division, and lead the division in tech.</div><div class="timestamp">01:19:17</div><div class="transcript-line">Because he was delayed and lost so many people, and because his division commander was pissed off at him for getting in a fight, this is what he tells me, when he came up there to get ready for Tet, he arrived too late and understrength, so he was third in line instead of first in line when they attacked Saigon. And he said to me, Colonel Welch, you saved my life. I would have been killed.</div><div class="timestamp">01:19:37</div><div class="transcript-line">And and why did you guys leave in Tet? You know, t you won in Tet. We didn&#39;t have anybody left.</div><div class="timestamp">01:19:44</div><div class="transcript-line">So we we can feel comfortable that uh it uh we&#39;re winning when we left. And I don&#39;t know what justification that is, but that uh it&#39;s not so it&#39;s not You just talked too much, Kerry. Well, I I&#39;d still like to ask one question unless you&#39;ve got uh Well I&#39;ve got about 18 more questions, but go ahead. Go ahead. Sure.</div><div class="timestamp">01:20:25</div><div class="transcript-line">I interviewed a artillery observer earlier in the this reunion. This is Andrew Woods from 1st Division Museum. And he had said, uh, you know, he was in one of the bird dog planes.</div><div class="timestamp">01:20:38</div><div class="transcript-line">And and he had said most battles he would direct artillery to fire on enemy troops But in this case they told him not to direct artillery on it. So so to me that says this is this is coming from higher up than the than the major than the major that&#39;s with the mortar platoon. And and it was his idea that this was some sort of West Point doctrine not to use artillery in this situation. The artillery that day was very strange because the enemy was uh three meters away. You know, the guys I was shooting at when things first started, they were shooting at us from the tree. And the trees are uh three feet away.</div><div class="timestamp">01:21:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I I sh I don&#39;t want to talk about me. I sh uh the first guy I shot with my forty five was right up there. And when I shot him, uh the blood from him came down and landed on my first side.</div><div class="timestamp">01:21:36</div><div class="transcript-line">He said, What the hell are you doing? I said, Well I shot that guy. And he fell out of the tree.</div><div class="timestamp">01:21:41</div><div class="transcript-line">So th they were You know, you can&#39;t fire artillery at a band that&#39;s right there like the Zealand. And you can&#39;t fire artillery at the and when I went, I I felt that they were too close. I knew about the mortars because you can see a mortar round come through the trees, who boom, and then you can say, okay, I want to bring that ten feet closer, you know, two meters, go right two meters, or whatever my stuff would be.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:04</div><div class="transcript-line">And with the artillery, we fired large artillery, was fired far away, 1155s. We had some of those because I could hear them. The ones I cared about were the 105s, which are really about to see. He&#39;s got 81 105s you can see a 105 come through the tree.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:18</div><div class="transcript-line">And Pinky Durham would be the one who&#39;d say, okay, I got that one. Move it two meters closer. Move it five meters closer.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:25</div><div class="transcript-line">Man, that&#39;s a. But now what artillery wants to do is shoot and you know adjust 500 meters. Drop 500. Well no, drop 500 wouldn&#39;t have done me any good.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:37</div><div class="transcript-line">So the The artillery, uh, I wanted it to fire all the time. And there&#39;s supposed to be some other mad magician up there that his stuff fires like that. Artillery fires like that, airplanes go like that, helicopters go like that.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:51</div><div class="transcript-line">Well you goddamn well ought to figure it out. Some son of a bitch up there should have been doing his job like we were doing ours. Instead of having somebody came come along and say, hey, this is too complicated, let&#39;s stop everything. Durham was calling in? It was two or three batteries, I believe. Okay. Now I never cared. What I cared about was I&#39;ve got one radio.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23:16</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;ve got a radio that goes to him and I got another radio goes to the artillery. And whether five, six, eight, or ten was shooting for me really didn&#39;t make any difference, except that I knew all of our artillery was coming from uh all of our mortars were coming from behind us, so prepare to shoot them on the left and right. And all of artillery was coming from directly to the uh east.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23:37</div><div class="transcript-line">So when the artillery, because the artillery does the same thing. Sometimes it&#39;s long, sometimes it&#39;s short, but it very seldom is off that way. So you fire the artillery in front of you and behind you, like we&#39;d done on the set 16th.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23:49</div><div class="transcript-line">It was really very, very simple. Now when it didn&#39;t work was just, I mean the plan was uh I mean the plan it&#39;s not that we did it on paper and didn&#39;t it it it should we should have done it like we did it on the 16th</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:24:02</div><div class="transcript-line">And the North Vietnamese weren&#39;t stupid. I mean if if if I&#39;m a North Vietnamese and you have a preponderance of artillery, my only chance is to move closer to you. to negate the the the amount of artillery you could bring in on me.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:24:15</div><div class="transcript-line">He said grab you by the belt buckle. Yeah oh that&#39;s what the phraseology I didn&#39;t have a belt buckle but he grabbed the front of my shirt said grab you by the belt buckle. And they did they were right there. And and uh when I would hear the colonel airstrikes, you know, gonna bring in some air. God damn, sir, what are you talking about Uh yeah, air is even further than artillery would be. A mile away. And and our enemy is uh ten feet away. So it&#39;s the mortal. It&#39;s the mortars you really needed.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:24:46</div><div class="transcript-line">And there&#39;s a just something I I recall about the mortars too. A lot of times we had fired mortars. This was triple canopy</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:24:52</div><div class="transcript-line">It was, it was really, I&#39;ve heard you get true. I just read one of the new things over there that talked about uh fairly open jungle. No, it was as solid as could be. It took us uh two hours to go uh thousand meters.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:25:04</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, there&#39;s a chance that waters are fired into that kind of tur or that kind of foliage that you get tree burst. In other words, the round would explode in the trees because it thinks it thinks it has impacted on the ground. So it&#39;s going to explode at the first solid contact.</div><div class="timestamp">01:25:19</div><div class="transcript-line">So then you have a a shrapnel spread that&#39;s you know all encompassing. But again, at that point, if your guys are getting overrun and they&#39;re you know in the monster wounded and stuff. Does it really, you know, it is it is it a negative to fire your own work, you know, sir?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:25:35</div><div class="transcript-line">As part of the uh when I met the enemy commander and we uh we stood there and I said, I think I was here. Looks different, but I I knew where we were I think we&#39;re here. And Colonel Triad looked at me and says, Colonel Welch, you were right here.</div><div class="timestamp">01:25:48</div><div class="transcript-line">And I said, good, and I understand. And I said, Colonel Triad, where were you? He said, over there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:25:56</div><div class="transcript-line">And I said, how far? He said about 60 meters. 60?</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:00</div><div class="transcript-line">60. 6-0.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:01</div><div class="transcript-line">And that was his command?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:02</div><div class="transcript-line">No, that was him saying that&#39;s where it now. And then Captain Lam says, I know where you were. Because the bunker, the command bunker, is still there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:09</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s not a bunker anymore, it&#39;s just where the dirt has sagged in because the big tractors go back and forth on it. So then when David Moranis read his stupid poem written by the Colonel&#39;s widow stupid poem written by the Colonel&#39;s widow that he read, even though we now know because we&#39;ve seen the film that the Colonel&#39;s widow kind of had something to do with all this. The uh uh Colonel Triette said, I will leave you Americans alone while you do this.</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:34</div><div class="transcript-line">I will measure the distance. So he takes off. 62 meters between me and the enemy regimental commander.</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:44</div><div class="transcript-line">Unheard of. It&#39;s impossible. Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:48</div><div class="transcript-line">62 meters. I mean there&#39;s 62 meters out here in the hallway. So regimental commanders there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:26:54</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m here. 62 meters So you&#39;re on top of him, but he&#39;s got a regiment and you&#39;ve got a He&#39;s been digging in for five days. There&#39;s two battalions on the right, one battalion on the left. We&#39;re here. He&#39;s there. This battalion on the left was uh uh as he told me after a couple minutes I saw that you guys weren&#39;t gonna move so I ordered them to and he stands up like this I ordered him to move in on you So the uh and then we said on that day all 1400 of us fired at the Americans.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:27:27</div><div class="transcript-line">And that under that&#39;s two under-strength companies.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:27:33</div><div class="transcript-line">Do you think it would be correct to say that you surprised each other? No. No, no. uh stuff about what happened in you know why would they let you in that close uh uh what colonel triet told me uh is they just wanted us to go. He said, I prayed to my God that you would turn around. And you did not turn around.</div><div class="timestamp">01:27:58</div><div class="transcript-line">You kept coming, you kept coming, you kept coming. I I know that&#39;s what I said. We&#39;re where he and I are standing there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:04</div><div class="transcript-line">Davorance is creeping in trying to write down stuff. And we can tell him to leave us alone. Said, you kept coming.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:10</div><div class="transcript-line">And then uh he said, I prayed to God, I prayed to my God that uh that you would turn around. But you kept coming, you kept coming, you kept coming. And finally I had to knock the two sticks together, which is in the book.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:24</div><div class="transcript-line">David Moratis made me promise that I wouldn&#39;t, because that we were on the ground and he heard that, so he wrote down, knock two sticks together, that&#39;s cool. And he made me promise afterwards not to ever tell anybody that the battle started by knocking two sticks together because that would be in his book. And that and anyway.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:40</div><div class="transcript-line">Now you can&#39;t. Yeah, now I can. But he said it. He said it.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:43</div><div class="transcript-line">He said uh I prayed to God that you turn around. All we wanted was food. We had to uh uh we were taking leaves off the trees, taking grass off the ground.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:53</div><div class="transcript-line">He said uh, I said, Colonel Triet, you&#39;re you had tough soldiers. He said, Colonel Welch, all soldiers are tough</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:00</div><div class="transcript-line">Andrew, they did cracking us since they left the NDP at 8 o&#39;clock. I bet you the the the movement of our guys at 8 o&#39;clock, probably they they were aware of it 801.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:09</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Sure yeah. Sure just another day for them.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:11</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:12</div><div class="transcript-line">They well they they were ever they were.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:13</div><div class="transcript-line">Because the Americans were there on the sixteenth and we&#39;re not leaving. You know, they knew we were staying there. They didn&#39;t see any helicopters coming in to evacuate us or to take us out. They knew the operation was still ongoing</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:22</div><div class="transcript-line">So they were they they were fully used so we could get the rice. Well, and you were going out there to find a main force, too. Yep.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:32</div><div class="transcript-line">In contrary of Clark or Lieutenant Welsh&#39;s best instincts.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:37</div><div class="transcript-line">We found him. Well, I think we&#39;re thank you very much. Thank you for your time. Yeah. You got a decompression room here. Thank you. Carrie, I&#39;ll I&#39;ll answer the rest of your questions.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:57</div><div class="transcript-line">I will too. This will probably be the one and only time. I know.</div>
